       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_98_v1'
from (values
('127.752.d.13.H',3,'A liquid gives off enough vapor to flash briefly at one temperature and ignites and continues burning at a higher one. This differentiates:','["wet and dry", "day and night", "flash point and ignition temperature", "hot and cold"]'::jsonb,2,'Flash point is lower.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.H',4,'The flash point is the lowest temperature at which a liquid:','["boils", "explodes", "freezes", "gives off enough vapor to ignite briefly"]'::jsonb,3,'Flash point = momentary ignition.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.I',3,'Gasoline floats on water because its specific gravity is less than 1. This is important because:','["burning liquids may spread on water", "fire is cooled", "water boils off", "gasoline sinks"]'::jsonb,0,'Floating fuel spreads fire.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.I',4,'A liquid with a specific gravity greater than 1 will:','["float", "sink in water", "evaporate", "burn"]'::jsonb,1,'Heavier than water sinks.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.J',3,'Propane vapors are heavier than air and collect in low areas. This is explained by:','["fuel color", "liquid density", "vapor density", "air speed"]'::jsonb,2,'Vapor density compares gas to air.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.J',4,'A vapor density greater than 1 means the vapor will:','["rise away", "disappear", "float up", "sink and collect in low areas"]'::jsonb,3,'Heavier than air.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.K',3,'A fire starts small, grows, reaches full burning, then declines. These are the:','["stages of fire development", "rules of ethics", "ranks of firefighters", "parts of a hose"]'::jsonb,0,'Incipient, growth, fully developed, decay.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.K',4,'The first stage of fire development is:','["flashover", "incipient", "fully developed", "decay"]'::jsonb,1,'Incipient is the beginning.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.L',3,'A sudden ignition of all room contents is a flashover, while a rush of air feeding a starved fire causes a backdraft. This differentiates:','["fuel and heat", "steam and fog", "flashover and backdraft", "smoke and ash"]'::jsonb,2,'Both are rapid fire events.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.L',4,'A backdraft is most likely when a firefighter opens a door to a room that is:','["full of water", "well ventilated", "cold and empty", "oxygen-starved and full of hot gases"]'::jsonb,3,'New air can cause backdraft.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.M',3,'Heat can move through a metal pipe, by rising hot air, and as waves across a gap. These are the three methods of heat transfer:','["conduction, convection and radiation", "rain, snow, wind", "walking, running, jumping", "day, night, dusk"]'::jsonb,0,'Three ways heat moves.','original'),
('127.752.d.13.M',4,'Feeling heat from a bonfire several feet away is an example of:','["conduction", "radiation", "combustion", "convection"]'::jsonb,1,'Radiation travels as waves.','original'),
('127.752.d.14.A',3,'A student lists ordinary combustibles, flammable liquids, energized electrical, combustible metals and cooking oils. These are the:','["three sides of a triangle", "four seasons", "five classes of fire (A, B, C, D, K)", "six types of hose"]'::jsonb,2,'Fire classes A, B, C, D, K.','original'),
('127.752.d.14.A',4,'A fire involving cooking oils and fats is a:','["Class B fire", "Class A fire", "Class C fire", "Class K fire"]'::jsonb,3,'K = kitchen.','original'),
('127.752.d.14.B',3,'Water cools Class A fires while foam smothers Class B fires. These are:','["extinguishing methods for the classes of fire", "shapes", "brands", "colors"]'::jsonb,0,'Each class has proper agents.','original'),
('127.752.d.14.B',4,'The best way to extinguish an ordinary wood fire is to:','["blow on it", "cool it with water", "cover with sugar", "smother it with oil"]'::jsonb,1,'Water cools Class A.','original'),
('127.752.d.15.A',3,'A city''s water system includes a source, treatment plant, pumps, storage tanks and mains. This identifies the:','["parts of a fire truck", "parts of a ladder", "basic components of a municipal water supply", "parts of a boat"]'::jsonb,2,'Municipal water supplies hydrants.','original'),
('127.752.d.15.A',4,'Elevated storage tanks in a water system help to:','["clean the streets", "launch boats", "cool the pipes", "maintain water pressure"]'::jsonb,3,'Gravity supports pressure.','original'),
('127.752.d.15.B',3,'In cold climates, the valve of a dry barrel hydrant is below ground, while a wet barrel hydrant holds water at all times. This identifies:','["types of fire hydrants", "types of hose", "types of ladder", "types of tool"]'::jsonb,0,'Dry barrel resists freezing.','original'),
('127.752.d.15.B',4,'Dry barrel hydrants are common in areas where:','["there is no water", "freezing temperatures occur", "boats are used", "it never rains"]'::jsonb,1,'They drain to prevent freezing.','original'),
('127.752.d.15.C',3,'Hydrants with certain colored caps tell firefighters their flow capacity quickly. This explains the:','["paint sale", "decoration", "purpose of hydrant color coding", "fashion"]'::jsonb,2,'Colors show flow.','original'),
('127.752.d.15.C',4,'A hydrant''s color coding helps firefighters know:','["its weight", "what year it was built", "who owns it", "how much water it can supply"]'::jsonb,3,'Color indicates flow rate.','original'),
('127.753.d.A',3,'A cadet sorts examples of a flat tire, a sudden overwhelming loss and a house fire into groups. This differentiates:','["a personal crisis, a problem and an emergency", "a rank, a badge and a patch", "a hose, a ladder and a pump", "a map, a key and a scale"]'::jsonb,0,'A problem is manageable; a crisis overwhelms coping; an emergency threatens safety now.','original'),
('127.753.d.A',4,'Which situation best fits a personal crisis?','["a missed bus", "a person overwhelmed after a sudden loss who cannot cope", "a spilled drink", "a flat tire"]'::jsonb,1,'A crisis overwhelms usual coping skills.','original'),
('127.753.d.B',3,'A trainer says a personal crisis usually involves a stressful event, a person''s perception of it, and an inability to cope. These are the:','["ranks of a station", "steps of a drill", "basic elements of a personal crisis", "parts of a hose"]'::jsonb,2,'Event + perception + failed coping.','original'),
('127.753.d.B',4,'Two people face the same event, but only one has a crisis because of how each:','["eats", "commutes", "dresses", "perceives it and copes"]'::jsonb,3,'Perception shapes crisis.','original'),
('127.753.d.C',3,'A counselor lists trauma, family conflict, job loss and illness as things that can trigger a crisis. These are:','["factors that may lead to a personal crisis", "fashion trends", "rewards", "hobbies"]'::jsonb,0,'Many stressors can lead to crisis.','original'),
('127.753.d.C',4,'Which is a factor that may contribute to a personal crisis?','["a sunny day", "the sudden death of a family member", "a new haircut", "a new pen"]'::jsonb,1,'Major losses can overwhelm coping.','original'),
('127.753.d.D',3,'A student reads how a crisis can affect mental health, emotions, relationships and work. This analyzes the:','["sizes of crises", "prices of crises", "impacts of personal crises", "colors of crises"]'::jsonb,2,'Crises affect many areas.','original'),
('127.753.d.D',4,'A prolonged personal crisis can impact a person by:','["improving sleep always", "raising grades", "removing all stress", "affecting health, relationships and performance"]'::jsonb,3,'Wide-ranging impact.','original'),
('127.753.d.E',3,'Talk of hopelessness, giving away belongings or threats to hurt oneself are warning signs that need:','["immediate attention", "no action", "waiting a year", "laughing off"]'::jsonb,0,'Serious warning signs need quick help.','original'),
('127.753.d.E',4,'If a coworker shows signs of being in immediate danger, the best step is to:','["gossip", "get help right away from trained responders or a crisis line", "post online", "wait and see"]'::jsonb,1,'Immediate danger needs immediate help.','original'),
('127.753.d.F',3,'Withdrawal, changes in sleep, drinking more, and missed shifts are signs that someone may need:','["a promotion", "a day off to travel", "intervention and support", "a bonus"]'::jsonb,2,'Indicators suggest a person may need help.','original'),
('127.753.d.F',4,'A coworker who suddenly stops joining the crew and misses shifts may need:','["punishment", "ridicule", "silence", "a caring check-in and connection to support"]'::jsonb,3,'Early support helps.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.A',3,'A responder offers safety, calm, connection and hope to a person after a disaster, following Psychological First Aid. This applies the:','["key principles of Psychological First Aid", "ladder rules", "hose lay steps", "rules of a card game"]'::jsonb,0,'PFA supports safety, calm, connection, self-efficacy and hope.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.A',4,'Psychological First Aid focuses on:','["punishment", "practical support and comfort for people in distress", "diagnosing disorders", "deep therapy"]'::jsonb,1,'PFA is early supportive care.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.B',3,'A responder guides an upset person to breathe in slowly, name five things they see, and feel their feet on the floor. These are:','["parade drills", "sports plays", "grounding techniques for crisis de-escalation", "dance moves"]'::jsonb,2,'Grounding returns attention to the present.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.B',4,'A slow breathing exercise helps a person in crisis by:','["increasing panic", "distracting from help", "raising heart rate", "calming the body''s stress response"]'::jsonb,3,'Slow breathing calms.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.C',3,'A responder speaks slowly, uses simple words, repeats the person''s name and avoids arguing. These are:','["communication techniques for crisis de-escalation", "ways to ignore", "ways to shout", "ways to hurry"]'::jsonb,0,'Calm, simple talk helps.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.C',4,'When de-escalating a person in crisis, a responder should:','["argue", "speak calmly and listen without judging", "threaten", "lecture"]'::jsonb,1,'Listening lowers tension.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.D',3,'A responder keeps hands open and visible, an open posture and a respectful distance. This shows how:','["standing over someone", "hiding hands", "body language supports de-escalation", "crossing arms"]'::jsonb,2,'Open body language signals safety.','original'),
('127.753.d.2.D',4,'Which body language is more likely to calm a distressed person?','["crowding", "clenched fists", "pointing", "relaxed shoulders, open hands and a respectful distance"]'::jsonb,3,'Nonthreatening posture reassures.','original'),
('127.753.d.3.A',3,'A fire chief lists the county mental health authority, a crisis response team and a local counselor''s line. These are:','["local crisis intervention resources", "food vendors", "car dealers", "travel agents"]'::jsonb,0,'Local services exist.','original'),
('127.753.d.3.A',4,'A mobile crisis outreach team can help a community by:','["fixing hoses", "responding to people in mental health crisis", "painting stations", "selling equipment"]'::jsonb,1,'They send trained clinicians.','original'),
('127.753.d.3.B',3,'The Texas Department of State Health Services offers programs and referrals for mental health and substance use help. This identifies:','["school rules", "parking permits", "state crisis resources", "tax forms"]'::jsonb,2,'DSHS provides statewide resources.','original'),
('127.753.d.3.B',4,'A statewide agency that can connect Texans to behavioral health services is:','["the Texas Racing Commission", "the Texas Library", "the Texas Lottery", "the Texas Department of State Health Services"]'::jsonb,3,'DSHS supports services.','original'),
('127.753.d.3.C',3,'A person in distress can call or text 988 for free, confidential help any time. This is a:','["national crisis intervention resource", "pizza hotline", "weather line", "bank number"]'::jsonb,0,'988 is the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline.','original'),
('127.753.d.3.C',4,'The national number to call or text for the Suicide and Crisis Lifeline is:','["311", "988", "511", "411"]'::jsonb,1,'988 connects to crisis counselors.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.A',3,'A veteran firefighter becomes irritable, has trouble sleeping and avoids the station after a hard call. These could be:','["signs of a promotion", "signs of luck", "warning signs of a mental health crisis in first responders", "signs of fitness"]'::jsonb,2,'Changes in mood and behavior warrant concern.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.A',4,'Which change might signal a mental health crisis in a first responder?','["new hobby only", "a day off", "a haircut", "withdrawing from friends and crew and losing interest in work"]'::jsonb,3,'Withdrawal is a warning sign.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.B',3,'After a child fatality call, a medic feels flashbacks, guilt and numbness. This analyzes the:','["psychological impacts of exposure to trauma", "benefits of hobbies", "effects of diet only", "effects of weather"]'::jsonb,0,'Trauma exposure has psychological effects.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.B',4,'Repeated exposure to trauma can lead first responders to experience:','["no reactions", "anxiety, intrusive thoughts and emotional numbness", "only boredom", "only joy"]'::jsonb,1,'Responders feel impacts too.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.C',3,'Headaches, stomach trouble, exhaustion and sleep problems can follow traumatic calls. These are:','["shift rewards", "lunch results", "physical impacts of trauma exposure", "luck signs"]'::jsonb,2,'Stress shows in the body.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.C',4,'Chronic trauma-related stress may physically affect a responder by causing:','["better vision", "bigger muscles", "more energy always", "fatigue and sleep problems"]'::jsonb,3,'Stress takes a physical toll.','original'),
('127.753.d.4.D',3,'A responder who is distressed after calls snaps at family, avoids friends and feels distant. This explains how trauma-related stress affects:','["relationships and social life", "weather", "tax rates", "vehicle speed"]'::jsonb,0,'Stress strains relationships.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
